= Entropy =

== Overview ==
'''Entropy''' is a family of quantitative concepts used to measure multiplicity, uncertainty, or dispersal under specific mathematical or physical formalisms.

Two major uses are thermodynamic/statistical-mechanical entropy and Shannon entropy in [[Information theory]].

They are mathematically related but should not be treated as one identical physical quantity.

== Statistical and thermodynamic entropy ==
In statistical mechanics, entropy is related to the number or probability distribution of microscopic states compatible with macroscopic conditions.

A Boltzmann form is:

<pre>
S = k_B ln W
</pre>

A Gibbs form is:

<pre>
S = -k_B Σ_i p_i ln p_i
</pre>

== Shannon entropy ==
For a discrete random variable X:

<pre>
H(X) = -Σ_x p(x) log p(x)
</pre>

It measures average uncertainty or average self-information.

The log base determines units such as bits or nats.

== Entropy and disorder ==
“Disorder” is a common intuition but not a universal definition.

Entropy can increase or decrease in situations where informal visual “disorder” is misleading.

Precise interpretation depends on the formalism and state space.

== Relationship between physical and informational entropy ==
Shannon and Gibbs entropies share the same logarithmic probability structure.

Their relationship is deep and useful.

However:

<pre>
Shannon entropy ≠ thermodynamic entropy by identity
</pre>

Physical entropy includes physical state definitions and units such as Boltzmann's constant.

== Related concepts ==
[[Information]], [[Information theory]], [[Probability distribution]], [[Uncertainty]], and [[Feedback]] provide neighboring mathematical/system concepts.

== Discrete information example ==
A fair binary variable with two equally likely results has one bit of Shannon entropy. If one result has probability one, its Shannon entropy is zero. These facts follow from the chosen probability model; measurement errors, uncertainty about the model and the consequences of an outcome are separate questions.

== Physical versus information-theoretic statements ==
[[Related to::Information theory|Information theory]] describes uncertainties and coding limits for defined probability spaces. Thermodynamic entropy refers to physically modeled macrostates and microscopic degrees of freedom, with units involving Boltzmann's constant. Mathematical similarity of the Gibbs and Shannon forms does not by itself establish equivalence between semantic information, computation, heat or physical work.

== Choice of coarse-graining and units ==
A numerical entropy value depends on the chosen states, probabilities, and logarithm base. Changing the state partition may alter the computed entropy. Comparisons between experiments or datasets must therefore declare the relevant microstate model or alphabet, probability assignments and units rather than treating entropy as a context-free measure of “disorder.”

== Entropy in thermodynamics ==
The second law states that the total thermodynamic entropy of an isolated system cannot decrease during a spontaneous process. An individual open subsystem can lose entropy while exchanging energy or matter with its environment; the surroundings must be included before applying the isolated-system statement. [[Related to::Thermodynamics|Thermodynamic entropy]] is a state property with physical units. The popular phrase “disorder always increases everywhere” is not an accurate general form of this law.

